# Workspace

This is a Remote Control session on the user's own VM, not a cloud session. Cloud-only tools (`add_repo`, `SendUserFile`, Projects, the cloud sandbox) don't exist here.

- Repositories live in `repos/<name>/`. If one isn't there, `git clone` it into `repos/`.
- Don't edit `repos/<name>/` directly. For each task, work in a worktree named after it: `git -C repos/<name> worktree add ../../worktrees/<name>-<branch> -b <branch>`. Reuse an existing worktree to continue earlier work, and remove it when the user says the task is finished.
- Keep repos current with `git -C repos/<name> pull --ff-only`: the first time you touch a repo in a session, and again whenever the user says something changed upstream (pushed, merged, a PR landed). New worktrees branch from the updated default branch. If it can't fast-forward, say so.
- Push to a branch and open a pull request with `gh pr create`. Never push to the default branch.
- Code you only want to read or try (third-party repos, untrusted downloads) goes in your scratchpad directory, never in `repos/` or `worktrees/`. Only `worktrees/` is trusted for `mise` tasks and the quality hooks, and the scratchpad is cleared when the container resets.

# Installing tools

- A missing CLI tool or runtime: install it to user space yourself before asking (no `brew`, `apt` or `sudo`). Prefer `mise` (`github:`, `aqua:`, `ubi:` backends), then `uv` or `npm` inside the project (venv, `node_modules`), not globally.
- Only the workspace and `~/.claude` survive a container restart. Record tools in the project's `mise.toml` with `mise use <tool>` in the project directory (not `-g`). If a tool you expect is missing, run `mise install` there first.
- Heavy native, CAD/CFD or CUDA stacks go in sibling Podman containers (limits and recipes: `/etc/claude-code/CLAUDE.md`).
- If an install fails on a blocked host, stop and name the host.

# Sharing with the user

Share reports, reviews and any document for the user to read as a **Claude Doc** (`mcp__claude_ai_Claude_Docs__*`), and give them the doc's link in your reply. A doc opens on desktop and mobile, while a file path only opens on desktop. The Docs tools' own instructions and `guide` cover how to build one. Docs here are standalone. claude.ai Projects (the chat-view feature with project knowledge and memory) aren't reachable from this session, so never try to add a doc to one or to its memory, and don't use `SendUserFile` or attachments. A `"kind":"project"` in a Docs tool call is just the doc itself, not a claude.ai Project.

- Images work. A doc takes only images uploaded to it. An image from a web address is refused (`unsupported_construct`), but that refusal doesn't mean images aren't supported. Add them last, once every section has its text and no pending block is left: (1) upload the local files with the `Artifact` tool (`action: "publish"`, `asset: true`, `url` = the doc's link, `file_paths` = absolute paths), which returns an asset id (32 hex, not the url); (2) create a blob in the doc for each asset id; (3) insert `![alt](blob/<blob id>)` after the caption paragraph. If a blob is rejected (`no blob with that id`, `not_minted`), the upload went to the wrong link: upload again to this doc's link. Read `guide(items=["topic.uploads"])` first.
- After filling a doc, leave one comment anchored on a passage you wrote, asking the user to confirm an assumption or choose between two options. No other unprompted comments.
